  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Core Feature**\n\nI\'ve got the basic dependencies sorted out. Now I\'m focusing on the user\'s specific request: an AI Studio System Instruction that will generate JSON with a patient response string. That\'s the core feature I\'m building!\n\n\n**Designing the UI Elements**\n\nOkay, the architectural foundation is in place! I\'m now sketching out the user interface details for both the patient and clinician views. For the patient, I\'m imagining a clean, intuitive interface with a prominent voice journal button and text entry option. Crisis triggers will immediately activate a full-screen, high-contrast modal with emergency contacts. On the clinician side, I\'m going for a structured grid to display \"Patient Cards\" derived from the JSON responses, showing key information like stressors and risk levels.\n\n\n**Refining the Audio Input**\n\nI\'ve realized the patient can either type or record a journal entry. Voice recording with the Web Audio API/MediaRecorder is viable! I\'m planning to convert the recorded audio to base64 inlineData. We can send this directly to the Gemini API, via POST request to `/api/analyze`, for transcription and analysis. This seems like a fully functional solution.\n\n\n**Refining the API Endpoint**\n\nI\'m now thinking about the API endpoint for audio or text input. I\'ll build a robust `/api/analyze-journal` endpoint to handle either. I\'m also planning to read `metadata.json` to customize the app\'s name and description. Since the app is currently untitled, I\'ll update it. However, the agent instructions say to keep the name strictly literal and descriptive, so I\'m thinking of naming it \"Empathetic Patient Triage & Support\".\n\n\n**Naming the Application**\n\nI\'ve decided on \"Mental Health Triage Platform\" for the application\'s name. I\'m now making sure the Express + Vite server requirements in the system instructions are properly implemented. I\'m verifying the `package.json` scripts and the `server.ts` configuration, specifically the API routes and Vite middleware. I am also checking on how the voice recording in the browser, to ensure the recorded audio Blob is converted to a base64 string, select the MIME type, and send it to the backend for processing via Gemini 3.\n\n\n**Optimizing Gemini Prompts**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on prompt engineering for Gemini Flash! I can send audio files directly, which is great. I\'m building a system instruction and a user prompt for text input. The instruction emphasizes empathy and structured JSON output for clinical triage. Good news: I can define `responseMimeType: \"application/json\"` and `responseSchema` in `@google/genai` to guarantee proper JSON! I\'m constructing the `responseSchema` based on the user\'s requirements.\n\n\n**Defining Response Structures**\n\nI\'m now refining the JSON schemas for both patient-facing validation and the clinician\'s triage summary. I am constructing the schemas to provide useful summaries and risk levels. I\'m focusing on the clinician summary, ensuring it contains key elements: the primary stressor, risk level (\"Low,\" \"Medium,\" or \"High\"), clinical notes, and the full transcript of what was said. The crisis trigger is set to boolean type.\n\n\n**Implementing the Gemini Call**\n\nI\'m now integrating the JSON schema into the Gemini call to ensure strict adherence to the defined structure. I\'m double-checking that the specified model, `\'gemini-3.5-flash\'`, is used for both text and audio processing, as it appears to be ideal for the task. I can directly pass the audio as base64 data within the `contents` property, ensuring efficient data transfer for transcription and analysis.\n\n\n**Integrating Multimodal Analysis**\n\nI\'m thrilled; Gemini\'s multimodal capabilities mean no separate transcription service is needed! It\'s one streamlined process for both text and audio analysis. I\'m providing a text entry option in case the browser lacks audio recording support. The UI design will be calming and mobile-first, with minimal soft colors. I\'m centering a large \'Record Voice Journal\' button and a text box below labeled \"Or type your journal here...\" for user-friendliness.\n\n\n**Enhancing Clinician Interactions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the clinician\'s view, aiming for a structured grid of \"Patient Triage Cards\" with assessments persisted in server-side memory for historical review. I will also incorporate a text-to-speech button to read the patient response and a calming design. The crisis modal\'s enhanced design uses high contrast with emergency contacts.\n\n\n**Storing Triage Assessments**\n\nI\'m now implementing persistent storage for patient triage assessments, retrieved via `/api/triage-history`. This is essential for a true two-sided platform. The assessments are saved in a server-side `assessments` array to preserve data across sessions, ensuring the clinician dashboard updates in real time. To provide an immediate demonstration of the platform\'s features, I\'ll add sample, diverse patient triage assessments on the backend to populate the clinician view with realistic examples.\n\n\n**Refining the Clinician View**\n\nI\'m working on the clinician view layout to offer a clean, organized dashboard. I\'ve got plans for a summary panel displaying total assessments, active crisis alerts, and risk level categorization. Filtering triage cards by risk levels is next. I\'m also planning a search bar for key terms and enhancing individual Patient Cards with verbatim transcripts, risk ratings, stressors, clinical notes, timestamps, and alert card background changes to reflect crisis triggers.\n\n\n**Implementing Escalation Handling**\n\nI\'m now integrating an escalation handling system for the clinician view. Crisis-level cards will display with a bold red border and a warning banner. I\'ll add buttons to acknowledge or mark escalated cases as contacted, updating the assessment status. To ensure the frontend runs properly, I\'ll review `src/App.tsx`, `tsconfig.json`, and `package.json` configurations. I\'m focusing on defining the server setup in `server.ts` and revising build scripts in `package.json`.\n\n\n"
