  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Constructing Server Infrastructure**\n\nI\'m now focused on building the `/server.ts` file. I\'m ensuring it precisely adheres to the specifications: setting up port 3000 and host 0.0.0.0. Additionally, I\'m integrating the essential full-stack middleware, including `express.json` and Vite middleware for development, to establish the foundational backend structure.\n\n\n**Defining API Endpoints**\n\nNow, I\'m concentrating on defining the API endpoints in `/server.ts`. I\'m integrating the Gemini API securely using `@google/genai`, including correct `httpOptions` and headers. Specifically, I\'m setting up `/api/analyze-journal`, `/api/triage-history`, and `/api/acknowledge-crisis`. I\'m working on how to handle the different content types, and what the `patient_response` should be. Also, I\'m starting to design a robust prompt for Gemini\'s multimodal capabilities, leveraging `gemini-3.5-flash` within `ai.models.generateContent`.\n\n\n**Refining Response Format**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the output format for the Gemini API responses. I\'m focusing on structuring the `clinician_summary` object. I am defining fields like `primary_stressor`, `risk_level`, `clinical_notes`, and `transcript`. I\'m also including a `crisis_trigger` boolean. I\'m considering the potential prompts needed to extract this information reliably. The format must be very specific. I am thinking of using the more appropriate `gemini-3.5-flash` for this, and will make sure the prompts are suitable for it. The goal is to provide a concise and useful response for the clinician. I\'m checking to make sure that the server does not need anything besides `@google/genai`, and so far, it does not!\n\n\n**Implementing API Key Handling**\n\nNow I\'m addressing the API key. I realized the code would crash if `GEMINI_API_KEY` is missing. To fix this, I\'m implementing lazy initialization with a `getGeminiClient()` function. This avoids immediate failure and allows for graceful handling if the key is not provided. I\'m also including the \'User-Agent\' header for the `httpOptions`.\n\n\n**Implementing Mock Database Data**\n\nI\'ve just considered seeding the database with mock assessment histories. I plan to include three scenarios: a low-risk case (work stress), a medium-risk case (grief/anxiety), and a high-risk scenario flagged with `crisis_trigger: true` (suicidal ideation). These mock entries will give immediate context to the Clinician view. And yes, I\'ll be implementing the `/api/analyze-journal` endpoint now.\n\n\n**Constructing the /api/analyze-journal Route**\n\nI\'m now designing the `/api/analyze-journal` route. I\'m focusing on handling text and audio inputs. I\'m logging requests and validating that either a `text` or `audio` field is present. For the audio option, I\'ll be converting the base64 data into an inlineData part with a dedicated prompt. I\'ve also constructed the system instruction for the Gemini API, ensuring it acts as both a patient-facing empathic assistant and a clinical data generator, outputting a strictly formatted JSON object. My intention is to include the  `responseMimeType: \"application/json\"`.\n\n\n**Defining Response Parameters**\n\nNow, I\'m defining the `responseSchema` precisely to guarantee a consistent JSON output. I\'ve specified `patient_response`, and a `clinician_summary` object with fields for `primary_stressor`, `risk_level` (ensuring values are \'Low\', \'Medium\', or \'High\'), `clinical_notes`, and a `transcript`. I\'ve made sure to provide detailed descriptions for each field in the schema to guide the LLM\'s response generation.\n\n\n**Handling API Failures**\n\nI\'m now integrating error handling within `/api/analyze-journal`. I\'m going to catch any errors from the Gemini API and return informative error messages to the client. I\'ll need to account for cases where the API key is missing or invalid. I\'m also preparing for potential API failures, ensuring the server doesn\'t crash but instead provides a useful response, including the `patient_response` with error information. I\'ll make sure the response is still returned as a JSON object of our schema. I need to make sure the response is still JSON.\n\n\n**Implementing API Key Fallback**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a graceful fallback for API key issues. If `GEMINI_API_KEY` is absent or the API call fails due to the key, I\'ll detect the error and respond by returning a friendly error message to the client, telling them to specify the API key. In the meantime, I will continue to provide a simulated response from a mock generation engine, that matches the schema, allowing the user\'s workspace preview to continue looking awesome even if the API key is not yet set. I must implement the real integration.\n\n\n**Integrating API Functionality**\n\nI\'m now integrating the actual Gemini API call within `/api/analyze-journal`, using a try-catch to manage potential errors from `@google/genai`. I\'ll return an informative HTTP 400 or 500 error, if the call fails because the key is missing or invalid, including instructions on setting the API key. Also, I will use a descriptive error message from the client. Finally, I will ensure a simulated mode or \"Test Mode\" is available so the user can see what results are like. I\'m focusing on making a graceful fallback.\n\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now create the file `server.ts` to implement our full-stack Express server. This server handles real-time audio and text journal uploads, proxies the requests to the Google Gemini API securely, maintains a history of patient assessments in memory (initialized with realistic cases), and provides the static file serving needed for local and production runtimes."
