  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Task 1**\n\nI\'ve got a solid grasp of the recording mechanisms. Task 1 is my current focus; I\'m dissecting the JavaScript requirements for microphone input capture using `navigator.mediaDevices`. The downsampling using `AudioContext` is key, I will focus on that aspect and develop a plan.\n\n\n**Refining Audio Processing**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the audio processing pipeline for Task 1. My focus is on achieving the 16,000 Hz, mono audio output demanded by Gemma 4. I\'ve confirmed that creating an `AudioContext` with `sampleRate: 16000` is the elegant, natively supported solution, handling downsampling directly. I can\'t wait to test this.\n\n\n**Confirming Native Downsampling**\n\nI\'ve just had a major realization! I initially considered a custom `ScriptProcessorNode` to handle downsampling to 16,000 Hz, but that\'s unnecessary. Because the `AudioContext` is created with `sampleRate: 16000`, the browser\'s audio processing layer *natively* downsamples the incoming audio stream to exactly 16,000 Hz. Extracting channel 0 with `getChannelData(0)` yields the required mono (1 channel) output, saving me a ton of work!\n\n\n**Implementing WAV Encoding**\n\nI\'ve got it! Downmixing is so straightforward now! I\'m moving on to generating a 16-bit PCM WAV Blob from the Float32 array of samples at 16,000 Hz. I\'m exploring writing a standard WAV binary encoder in JavaScript. I will now define this helper function/object to convert this raw data into the necessary WAV format, and plan to implement it next.\n\n\n**Finalizing WAV Encoding**\n\nI\'ve got the WAV encoder implemented and it works beautifully! The solution is lightweight, fully in-memory, highly performant, and doesn\'t rely on any external NPM dependencies! The encoder\'s design translates Float32Arrays into 16-bit PCM WAV format. I\'m especially pleased with its direct compatibility for backend transmission, a key aspect I considered throughout the process. I am now confident that this will work with the backend.\n\n\n**Preparing Backend Interaction**\n\nI\'m now getting ready to send the WAV audio data to the backend! My immediate task is to encode the Blob into a Base64 string for transmission. I\'ve also started looking into the server\'s `server.ts` file to see how it will process the Base64 audio string. I need to make sure I am doing what the instructions call for. I\'m now examining the server\'s Gemini 4 schema instructions. I need to make sure I set the model to `gemma-4-e4b-it`.\n\n\n**Verifying API Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating with the Google Generative AI SDK. I\'m confirming that the model string `\'gemma-4-e4b-it\'` is passed to the Google backend exactly as it is received from the user, in accordance with the prompt\'s instructions. Also, I\'ve confirmed I can pass audio inline as a `audio/wav` string using the `@google/genai` SDK `contents` array as `inlineData`. I\'m verifying the backend uses it.\n\n\n**Adapting Mime Type**\n\nI\'ve got a key update on the audio data transmission. I was just reviewing the image format and realized I can adapt the provided structure for audio. The critical change is setting `mimeType` to `\"audio/wav\"` and using the `inlineData` property for the Base64 audio string, which is clean and compliant. This aligns perfectly with the instructions.\n\n\n\n**Validating Schema Integration**\n\nI\'m now completely focused on the backend integration and verifying the JSON schema. It is now fully typed and uses the correct `Type` definitions from `@google/genai`. I\'ve confirmed that the schema uses the `Type.OBJECT`, `Type.STRING`, and `Type.BOOLEAN` imports from the SDK. It\'s now fully compliant with the SDK.\n\n\n"
