  planner_response {
    modified_response: "I have successfully configured **SwaraSaathi** with the requested features, including the addition of a confidential clinical score and a detailed guide on how to integrate and provision Gemma on Google Cloud Platform\'s (GCP) Vertex AI.\n\nHere is an architectural map of how everything is structured, what happens to each audio recording, and how you can configure Vertex AI in your Google Cloud Project.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 1: How the App Works & Audio Processing Chain\n\nEvery audio recording submitted in **SwaraSaathi** undergoes a structured processing chain to guarantee strict clinical accuracy, fast network transport, and model support:\n\n1. **Client-Side Acoustic Pre-Processing (Vanilla JS)**\n   - When you tap the microphone, it accesses user authorization using `navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia`.\n   - The stream is routed to the browser\'s **AudioContext API**, where it is downsampled to **16,000 Hz** and downmixed to **1 mono channel** using a custom `ScriptProcessorNode`. This matches open-model acoustic standards (e.g., Gemma and Speech-to-Text inputs) perfectly.\n   - The raw 32-bit float sample arrays are encoded into a standard binary **16-bit PCM WAV Blob** on the fly, converted to Base64, and POSTed to the Express server.\n\n2. **Server-Side Clinical Triage & Gemma Analysis**\n   - The Express server acts as a secure api proxy, meaning **your API Key is never revealed to the client browser**.\n   - For every audio or journal entry received, the backend requests the model using the strict formatting schema:\n     - **Verbatim Transcript**: A transcripts array transcribing the speech verbatim.\n     - **Summarization & Formulation**: Extracts the primary psychological stressor (e.g., *Work Burnout*, *Family Stress*) and writes structured, professional clinical formulation notes for medical practitioners.\n     - **Categorization & Crisis Evaluation**: Classes the patient\'s immediate risk level (**Low**, **Medium**, or **High**) and flags the `crisis_trigger` boolean. If true (detecting immediate self-harm or active danger), it immediately locks the patient interface with an empathetic, supportive intervention modal displaying emergency resources/support hotlines.\n     - **Confidential Distress Score (Newly Added)**: Resolves an objective clinical distress rating ranging from **1** to **100**. This score is displayed strictly on the clinician\'s authenticated triage portal and **is completely hidden from the patient\'s view** to follow optimal professional guidelines.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 2: Deploying & Enabling Gemma on GCP Vertex AI\n\nTo move from the default Google AI Studio API sandbox to a dedicated Google Cloud Vertex AI endpoint with Gemma, follow these steps in your GCP project (**`gen-lang-client-0740135630`**):\n\n#### Step 1: Enable the Vertex AI API\n1. Open the [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com).\n2. Ensure you have selected your project: **`gen-lang-client-0740135630`**.\n3. Go to the **API Library** page and search for **Vertex AI API** (`aiplatform.googleapis.com`).\n4. Click **Enable**.\n\n#### Step 2: Deploy Gemma from Vertex AI Model Garden\n1. Go to the **Vertex AI Dashboard** in your Cloud Console.\n2. Select **Model Garden** on the left menu.\n3. Search for **Gemma** or **Gemma 2** (the highly efficient open models developed by Google).\n4. Select the model size that fits your latency/budget requirements (e.g., `Gemma 2 9B` or `Gemma 2 27B`).\n5. Click **Deploy** to create an online endpoint:\n   - Configure your machine type (e.g., NVIDIA L4, T4 or A100/H100 GPUs based on deployment pricing).\n   - Once successfully provisioned, Google Cloud will provide a dedicated **Endpoint ID** like:  \n     `projects/gen-lang-client-0740135630/locations/<region>/endpoints/<endpoint-id>`.\n\n#### Step 3: Server Authentication & Google Cloud SDK Integration\nWhen running in production on Google Cloud Run (our hosting sandbox):\n* Rather than maintaining static API keys, your server container authenticates seamlessly using **Google Application Default Credentials (ADC)**.\n* Give the Cloud Run container\'s Service Account the **Vertex AI User** (`roles/aiplatform.user`) role in the GCP IAM console.\n* Swap the `@google/genai` dependency client to use Vertex AI endpoints directly inside `server.ts` like so:\n\n```ts\nimport { GoogleGenAI } from \"@google/genai\";\n\n// Standard Vertex AI initialization without static keys (reads default credentials)\nconst ai = new GoogleGenAI(); \n\n// Call the model utilizing your newly created Vertex AI project parameters\nconst response = await ai.models.generateContent({\n  // Point to vertex-specific model formats or custom deployed Model Garden endpoints\n  model: \'projects/gen-lang-client-0740135630/locations/us-central1/endpoints/<your-endpoint-id>\',\n  contents: parts,\n  config: {\n    systemInstruction,\n    responseMimeType: \'application/json\',\n    responseSchema\n  }\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n### Phase 3: PWA & Service Worker Functionality\n\n1. **`manifest.json`**: Mounted cleanly inside the browser, certifying **SwaraSaathi** is installable on mobile and desktop platforms with custom splash colors and descriptive assets.\n2. **`sw.js` (Service Worker)**: Registers dynamic offline-caching layers for all static resources (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, assets) with a clean network-fallback retry strategy.\n"
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
